d-record holder at 10,000 metres and in the one-hour run, he was considered someone to watch. The London organizers chose not to run over the point-to-point course used for the " [:a {:href "/results/56369"} "1908 Olympics"] ", which had established the standard marathon distance of 42.195 km (26 miles, 385 yards). Instead, the race started and finished at " [:a {:href "/venues/339"} "Wembley Stadium"] ", looping thru the London suburbs."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Raceday was cloudy and humid with some wind, with the event starting at 3 PM. The immediate lead was taken by the Belgian " [:a {:href "/athletes/65193"} "Étienne Gailly"] " in his marathon début. He held the lead almost the entire race, but was passed briefly at about 35 km by the Korean " [:a {:href "/athletes/72913"} "Choi Yun-Chil"] ". However, Gailly regained the lead quickly and held it as he entered Wembley Stadium. Unfortunately, he had given all he had to that point. He was wobbling and stumbling along the track, evoking memories of " [:a {:href "/athletes/72093"} "Dorando Pietri"] " in 1908. On the track, he was passed first by Argentina’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/64400"} "Delfo Cabrera"] ", who had run a steady pace moving up throughout, and then by Britain’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/69432"} "Tom Richards"] ". Cabrera won the gold medal, with Richards second. Gailly was able to finish the race, and held on for a bronze medal. He had never gone beyond 18 miles in training but, before the race, he said, “If I am still standing at the finish I will have won a medal”. Gailly did both but only barely."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The following morning, at 7.30 AM, Richards was back at his work as a psychiatric nurse at a hospital around 15 miles from the stadium."] "\n"] [:button {:id "button672310", :class "btn btn-default"} "Details"] [:script {} "$('#button672310').click(splits)"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Time"